is for her and it's for our family.

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:08.318
- (Jenny): We were in a small town.

00:01:09.319 --> 00:01:11.404
It was really tough as a single parent.

00:01:13.031 --> 00:01:15.658
Having interracial children made it a lot worse.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:18.161
I had a black boyfriend and

00:01:18.161 --> 00:01:19.412
an interracial child 

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:21.831
at the time, and it just wasn't

00:01:21.831 --> 00:01:23.041
accepted.

00:01:30.048 --> 00:01:32.884
Nevada forwards Cody and Caleb Martin

00:01:32.884 --> 00:01:34.886
grew up in Cooleemee,  North Carolina,

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:37.514
a town located about 40 minutes southwest

00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:39.432
of Winston-Salem.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.561
Once a thriving mill village,

00:01:43.561 --> 00:01:44.521
the area is known 

00:01:44.521 --> 00:01:46.481
for its deep-seated racism.

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:56.533
- You wake up, it's a Sunday morning, and you look outside.

00:01:56.533 --> 00:01:57.534
There's no one there.

00:01:57.534 --> 00:01:58.535
There's no one out there, 

00:01:58.535 --> 00:02:00.036
but there's a cross that,

00:02:00.036 --> 00:02:01.037
that was

00:02:01.037 --> 00:02:02.789
burned.

00:02:03.206 --> 00:02:05.583
You know, the thoughts that are going through your mind are,

00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:07.043
How did this happen?&nbsp;

00:02:07.043 --> 00:02:08.044
You don't even know.

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.631
- Were you at any point fearful of, not only just your life

00:02:11.631 --> 00:02:13.133
but also your kids' lives?

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:14.008
- Um...

00:02:14.008 --> 00:02:15.718
a little bit, like, not-

00:02:15.718 --> 00:02:17.262
I was and I wasn't.

00:02:17.262 --> 00:02:18.513
You know, you just don't know 

00:02:18.513 --> 00:02:20.265
&lt;i&gt;what's, what could happen.&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:20.265 --> 00:02:21.516
Yeah, it was scary.

00:02:22.809 --> 00:02:23.893
You're thinking, what's next?

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:24.894
Are they going to 

00:02:24.894 --> 00:02:26.437
set your house on fire, or

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:27.981
you know, will they try 

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:28.857
to do something to your children

00:02:28.857 --> 00:02:29.816
when you're not there?

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.071
- (Caleb): Predominantly, like, a racist area

00:02:35.071 --> 00:02:36.406
just because

00:02:36.406 --> 00:02:37.240
it's a small town,

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:37.991
so everybody knows everything.

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:39.576
A mix between races-

00:02:39.576 --> 00:02:42.328
it was just kind of crazy how, like, you go from

00:02:42.328 --> 00:02:44.539
you weren't accepted because you were with a black guy

00:02:44.539 --> 00:02:45.498
in a predominantly white area.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:46.916
Then, you know, at first.

00:02:46.916 --> 00:02:48.126
Then you're like

00:02:48.126 --> 00:02:49.210
kind of an outcast 

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:50.753
because you're the one white parent

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:51.963
around a predominantly black area.

00:02:57.177 --> 00:02:59.220
- When you hear her telling you stories about 

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.180
a burning cross

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:01.181
as she wakes up

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:02.640
or walks outside of her door,

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:04.392
what goes through your mind?

00:03:05.810 --> 00:03:07.187
- Kind of pisses you off

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:07.812
- Kind of pisses you off
- Yeah.

00:03:07.812 --> 00:03:09.063
a little bit.

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:11.149
Just, just because, 

00:03:11.149 --> 00:03:12.108
and dealing with discrimination,

00:03:12.108 --> 00:03:13.109
like, all this stuff, 

00:03:13.109 --> 00:03:14.652
it's like

00:03:14.652 --> 00:03:16.487
people really just add on to her plate.

00:03:16.487 --> 00:03:17.530
And it's, like,

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:18.489
for her to really

00:03:18.489 --> 00:03:19.532
fight through adversity

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.742
and kind of persevere, like it's,

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:22.160
it's crazy

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:23.578
to see her do that.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:24.871
- (Jenny): These are people

00:03:24.871 --> 00:03:27.040
that live in the same community that you live in, and

00:03:27.624 --> 00:03:28.625
some are ones 

00:03:28.625 --> 00:03:29.792
that look at you

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:30.627
day to day.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:31.961
And um,

00:03:31.961 --> 00:03:32.962
you know...

00:03:33.963 --> 00:03:35.506
but it was very scary.

00:03:36.716 --> 00:03:39.010
We were just driving, and they just try to

00:03:39.844 --> 00:03:40.929
swerve and make you just

00:03:40.929 --> 00:03:42.096
run off the road.

00:03:42.096 --> 00:03:43.306
And they're

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:44.307
throwing out slurs

00:03:44.307 --> 00:03:45.308
or whatever out the window,

00:03:45.308 --> 00:03:46.309
and then, you know,

00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:47.310
kind of swerved to

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:49.020
like, try to run you over

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:50.104
while you are walking.

00:03:50.104 --> 00:03:51.022
And you pretty much have to

00:03:51.022 --> 00:03:52.690
kind of jump over to a side and

00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:54.943
like, this is ridiculous.

00:03:54.943 --> 00:03:56.069
- We had to 

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:57.487
take the trash out in pairs.

00:03:57.862 --> 00:03:58.947
We had to take the trash out in twos

00:03:58.947 --> 00:04:00.240
to make sure nothing happened,

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:01.449
make sure we were safe.

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:02.784
- You try to put it back,

00:04:02.784 --> 00:04:04.452
in the back of your mind and not think about it,

00:04:04.452 --> 00:04:05.161
not dwell on it,

00:04:05.161 --> 00:04:05.954
but

00:04:05.954 --> 00:04:07.455
you just know hatred was real.

00:04:07.455 --> 00:04:08.414
It is real,

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:09.415
and um 

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:10.583
you just have to 

00:04:10.583 --> 00:04:12.919
prepare your children for that.

00:04:15.838 --> 00:04:18.007
- A prejudiced environment wasn't the only challenge

00:04:18.007 --> 00:04:18.925
Jenny Bennett

00:04:18.925 --> 00:04:20.385
and her three children faced. 

00:04:20.927 --> 00:04:22.679
The family lived in poverty.

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:24.806
And feared for its life,

00:04:24.806 --> 00:04:25.890
surrounded by violence

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.141
related to drug dealing.

00:04:29.811 --> 00:04:31.229
Sacrifices were made

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:32.522
that the Martin twins didn't realize

00:04:32.522 --> 00:04:34.232
until they were older.

00:04:35.817 --> 00:04:37.193
- (Caleb): Insects, the bugs and stuff

00:04:37.193 --> 00:04:38.361
in the trailer

00:04:38.361 --> 00:04:39.487
and not having any money

00:04:39.487 --> 00:04:40.446
and, like,

00:04:40.446 --> 00:04:41.906
not even realizing that, you know,

00:04:41.906 --> 00:04:43.116
that my mom's not eating

00:04:43.116 --> 00:04:44.826
so we can eat.

00:04:45.785 --> 00:04:46.953
- (Jenny): I was Section 8,

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.955
and we had a single-wide traler and

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.164
it was-

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:54.377
It had roaches, and

00:04:54.377 --> 00:04:56.004
there was rats.

00:04:56.004 --> 00:04:57.338
It was awful.

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:01.342
I had two or three jobs.

00:05:01.342 --> 00:05:02.927
And um,

00:05:02.927 --> 00:05:04.721
and I just, you know,

00:05:04.721 --> 00:05:06.306
I did what I had to do.

00:05:06.306 --> 00:05:07.557
And,

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.143
and you know

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:11.185
sometimes I didn't eat.

00:05:11.185 --> 00:05:13.104
-(Caleb): She would say, "I'm not hungry."

00:05:13.104 --> 00:05:14.105
All day she wouldn't eat.

00:05:14.105 --> 00:05:15.481
She'd say, "I'm not hungry."

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:16.899
And then we'd eat, and then

00:05:16.899 --> 00:05:18.026
there wouldn't be nothing left

00:05:18.026 --> 00:05:18.860
so then she just, like,

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:19.569
wouldn't eat.

00:05:19.569 --> 00:05:21.029
-(Cody): She's the one who's working.

00:05:21.029 --> 00:05:21.946
She's the one making the money,

00:05:21.946 --> 00:05:22.905
and she's coming back,

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:23.990
paying for it.

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:25.658
And she's not even eating it.

00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:27.118
So it's, like, that's stuff you don't notice.

00:05:27.118 --> 00:05:28.119
And you look back,

00:05:28.119 --> 00:05:29.120
you're like, "Dang".

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.079
Like,

00:05:30.079 --> 00:05:31.247
that's why there's so much appreciation

00:05:31.247 --> 00:05:32.123
and love for my mom

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:33.124
and putting us first.

00:05:33.124 --> 00:05:33.916
That was

00:05:33.916 --> 00:05:34.709
so unselfish.

00:05:38.129 --> 00:05:40.381
- Growing up around racism and poverty,

00:05:40.381 --> 00:05:42.592
the Martin twins needed an outlet.

00:05:47.388 --> 00:05:49.515
What role did basketball have in their childhood?

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.684
- It kept them focused,

00:05:51.684 --> 00:05:52.894
kept them motivated.

00:05:52.894 --> 00:05:54.562
Gave them an out,

00:05:54.562 --> 00:05:56.356
and that was a release for them.

00:06:00.902 --> 00:06:02.695
- (Cody): Growing up, we had to figure out a way 

00:06:02.695 --> 00:06:04.322
to help her out at some point, and

00:06:04.322 --> 00:06:05.823
that's when we got first jobs.

00:06:05.823 --> 00:06:08.076
During basketball season, we worked at Walmart.

00:06:08.076 --> 00:06:09.160
We were cart-pushers.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:10.661
We wouldn't make a lot of money,

00:06:10.661 --> 00:06:11.996
but, like, we'd make enough

00:06:11.996 --> 00:06:12.872
to where we could pay for

00:06:12.872 --> 00:06:13.539
gas and food

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:14.874
so kind of take something off her plate.

00:06:14.874 --> 00:06:16.584
- It brought them closer together,

00:06:16.584 --> 00:06:17.585
and yeah, it wasn't

00:06:17.585 --> 00:06:19.337
the best of situations, but

00:06:20.546 --> 00:06:22.006
I think that's what made them,

00:06:22.924 --> 00:06:24.175
cuz they are really close.

00:06:24.467 --> 00:06:25.134
I love that.

00:06:25.134 --> 00:06:26.636
I love that they love each other.

00:06:26.636 --> 00:06:27.595
When they all get together

00:06:27.595 --> 00:06:28.721
they still wrestle around to the floor

00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:29.931
as old as they are.

00:06:29.931 --> 00:06:30.932
It's ridiculous.

00:06:32.016 --> 00:06:36.104
(Laughing)

00:06:38.189 --> 00:06:39.315
(Laughing)

00:06:39.315 --> 00:06:41.025
I wouldn't have it any other way.

00:06:42.068 --> 00:06:43.236
The reality is

00:06:44.529 --> 00:06:47.073
they couldn't have anybody love them more than me,

00:06:47.073 --> 00:06:48.074
for sure.

00:06:49.158 --> 00:06:51.577
- How much does that, looking back on it now, mean to you?

00:06:51.577 --> 00:06:52.995
Everything.

00:06:53.371 --> 00:06:54.872
Everything, cuz

00:06:54.872 --> 00:06:57.959
I am who I am because of them.

00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:18.229
I don't know where I would be without them.

00:07:20.189 --> 00:07:22.108
For sure.

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:30.032
I told you don't make me cry.

00:07:30.032 --> 00:07:31.534
(Laughing)

00:07:32.034 --> 00:07:34.036
- (Announcer): Mom, Jenny Bennett,

00:07:34.036 --> 00:07:36.581
cheering on this Wolf Pack comeback!

00:07:36.998 --> 00:07:37.999
- That's why we go so hard.

00:07:37.999 --> 00:07:38.958
And I think that's why we,

00:07:39.125 --> 00:07:40.126
you know, when we play,

00:07:40.126 --> 00:07:41.127
we play with a lot of passion.

00:07:41.127 --> 00:07:43.004
and we're trying to hopefully put ourselves and

00:07:43.337 --> 00:07:44.964
my mom and our family

00:07:44.964 --> 00:07:46.174
in the best situation

00:07:46.174 --> 00:07:48.718
to where they don't have to worry about anything anymore.

